The work presented here describes aggregation properties and thermoresponsive behavior of the two biopolymers under different conditions using measurements of turbidimetry, differential scanning calorimetry, and circular dichroism. We extended this investigation to the employment of HELPs for surface modification, which explored the potential of these biopolymers for adapting surfaces suitable for cell growth.

Recently, biofunctional protein polymers based on the pentapeptidic VPGXG motif have been proposed as a new approach to prepare neural implant biointerfaces.25

Indeed, biomaterials with smart properties and controlled features at the micro- and nanolevel are expected to play an important role in neural tissue repair and regeneration.25-27 In particular, surface engineering approaches for neural cell adhesion and the related cell response have received significant attention.28 HELPs possess several desirable properties that hold promise for development of biomaterials that can be employed in neural repair and regeneration strategies. Being proteins, they are of biotic origin and they are stable and biodegradable without release of toxic degradative compounds; their modular structure allows tuning of their properties and functionalization with bioactive domains. Moreover, their smart properties represent an advantage when fabrication techniques (e.g., controlled deposition, self-assembling, etc.) could be applied.

As a first step, in this study, we assessed the cytocompatibility of HELP biopolymers. For this purpose, coatings were developed and assayed with SH-SY5Y, a neuroblastic subclone widely used as a neuronal cell model, with the objective of developing biocompatible matrices to employ in neural tissue engineering.

# Materials and Methods

Expression and Purification of HELPs. E. coli competent cells were transformed with the expression constructs bearing HELP or HELP1 synthetic genes.24 Typically, \(80\;\mathrm{mL}\) of Terrific broth with 100 \(\mu\mathrm{g/mL}\) of ampicillin were grown overnight at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Cells collected by centrifugation were resuspended in \(800\;\mathrm{mL}\) of fresh medium and grown at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under shaking conditions until turbidity at \(600\ \mathrm{nm}\) reached about 1. Culture was then induced with IPTG to a final concentration of \(0.1\;\mathrm{mM}\) and allowed to grow further for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) . Induced bacterial cells were collected by centrifugation and eventually stored at \(-80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . Extraction and purification of broth recombinant polypeptides was carried on as already described.25,26 After purification, the material was dialyzed in cold water and then lyophilized for long-term storage.

Sodium Dodecyl Sulfate-Polyacrylamide Gel Electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE). The procedure was the same described previously.24 Briefly, recombinant protein samples were analyzed by SDS-PAGE performed in a vertical electrophoresis unit (CBS Scientific Co., U.S.A.). Protein solutions were mixed with reducing SDS-sample buffer. Molecular weight markers used were: \(66\;\mathrm{kDa}\) , bovine serum albumine; \(45\;\mathrm{kDa}\) , ovalbumin, \(30\;\mathrm{kDa}\) , carbonic anhydrase. After the run, the gel was stained with Coomassie Brilliant Blue G-250. Gels were photographed by using Gel Doc 2000 documentation system (Bio-Rad).

Reversed-Phase High-Performance Liquid Chromatography (RP-HPLC). RP-HPLC was performed on a C18 RP-column \((15~\times\) \(0.46~\mathrm{cm}\) , Phenomenex, Torrance, CA, U.S.A.) equilibrated with \(5\%\) acetonitrile \([0.1\%\) Trifluoroacetic acid, TFA). The column was eluted at \(0.75\,\mathrm{\mL/min}\) with a \(20\,\mathrm{\min}\) linear gradient from \(5\%\) acetonitrile \([0.1\%\) TFA) to \(80\%\) acetonitrile \([0.1\%\) TFA). The eluate was monitored with a UV spectrophotometer at \(230\ \mathrm{nm}\) .

Turbidimetry. The inverse transition temperatures of HELP and HELP1 samples were determined by measuring the transmission \((T)\)

at \(350\ \mathrm{nm}\) as a function of temperature at a heating scan rate of 1 \(^\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C/m i n}\) on a Varian DMS80 spectrophotometer. The transmission data were converted to turbidity percentage as \((1\mathrm{~-~}T)\times100\) . The inverse transition temperature \((T_{\mathrm{t}})\) was defined as the temperature corresponding to \(50\%\) of the maximum value. Purified proteins were dissolved in Tris buffer ( \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) , \(\mathrm{pH}=8\) ) in the concentration range \(0.5{-}5~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) and equilibrated for \(16~\mathrm{h}\) at \(4~^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) , in the absence or in the presence of a proper concentration of NaCl.

Differential Scanning Calorimetry. The thermal properties of HELP biopolymers were assessed using a Sclaram Micro-DSC III. DSC aluminum cells were filled by weight with protein samples \((4~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) , \(0.05{-}0.9\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{NaCl}\) concentration range, \(10\;\mathrm{mM}\) Tris buffer, \(\mathrm{pH}=8\) ), hermetically sealed and equilibrated for \(16\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) . The calorimetric program consisted of a pre-equilibration phase at \(20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{\min}\) and then heating from 20 to \(50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a scan rate of \(0.5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C/min}\) . A solvent containing cell was used as reference. Peak temperature \((T_{\mathrm{pk}})\) was evaluated as the maximum of heat absorption. Enthalpy \((\Delta H_{\mathrm{tr}})\) and entropy \((\Delta S_{\mathrm{tr}})\) of transition were obtained by area integration using a homemade graphic software.

Circular Dichroism. HELP and HELP1 were dissolved at concentrations of \(0.1\ \mathrm{mg/mL}\) in water alone and in the presence of \(0.15\mathrm{~M~}\) NaCl. The same solutions were analyzed adding \(50\%\) 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol (TFE, Sigma Aldrich). CD spectra were recorded at 10, 23, and \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a thermostatic cell from 190 to \(260\;\mathrm{nm}\) on a Jasco J-710 Spectrometer under constant nitrogen flush. An external bath was used for temperature control and the temperature was stepwise increased allowing the samples to equilibrate. CD data are expressed as mean residue molar ellipticity \([\theta]\) in degrees \((\mathrm{cm}^{2}\cdot\mathrm{dmol})\) .

Secondary Structure Evaluations. The grand average of hydropathy value (GRAVY) for a peptide or protein was calculated as the sum of hydropathy values of all the amino acids divided by the number of residues in the sequence by using the ProtParam (Expasy) program.29

Prediction of secondary structures of HELP and HELP1 was based on the primary amino acid sequences of the polypeptides using GOR IV and SOPMA available through the Expasy Web site (http:// www.au.expasy). GOR IV is the fourth version of GOR secondary structure prediction methods based on the information theory30 that uses all possible pair frequencies within the window of 17 amino acid residues. SOPMA (Self-Optimized Prediction Method with Alignment) is based on the homologue method.31

Coatings and Cell Culture Conditions. The SH-SY5Y human neuroblastoma cell lines (kind gift of Dr. E. Tongiorgi, University of Trieste, Italy) were routinely grown in Dulbecco's modified Eagle's medium (DMEM) supplemented with \(2~\mathrm{mM}\) L-glutamine, \(100\;\mu\mathrm{g/mL}\) streptomycin, and 100 units/mL penicillin and containing \(10\%\) (v/v) heat-inactivated fetal calf serum (FCS, Eukyclone). Cells were maintained at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a saturated humidity atmosphere containing \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in \(25~\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) flasks.

Lyophilized samples of biopolymers were dissolved in bidistilled cold water at a concentration of \(0.5\mathrm{~mg/mL}\) and sterilized by a 0.22 \(\mu\mathrm{m}\) filter. Wells \((0.32\;\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) of 96-well microtiters (BD Falcon, tissue culture treated by vacuum gas plasma or Sacco s.r.l., untreated polystyrene microtiters) were filled with \(25\;\;\mu\mathrm{L}\) of the sterilized biopolymer solution and let dry under sterile hood flow at room temperature \((22\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . In these conditions, the coatings consisted of about \(370~\mathrm{ng}\) per square millimeter. Control wells were filled with the same volume of sterile water. Cells were seeded at 10000 cells per well in a final volume of \(200~\mu\mathrm{L}\) of medium.

Differentiation experiments were carried on in tissue culture-treated 96-well microtiters (BD Falcon, vacuum gas plasma treated) following the same coating procedure. To differentiate SH-SY5Y, \(10\,\mu\mathrm{M}\) retinoic acid was added in the culture \(24~\mathrm{h}\) after seeding. Cells were cultured for an additional \(96~\mathrm{h}\) to observe elongation of neurites.

Each sample was replicated at least three times. Samples were observed through phase contrast microscopy (Leica) and photographed.
