A striking difference was observed when all of the artificial polypeptides and the recombinant HELPs were compared to the bovine and human elastin proteins, which showed half the value of the hydropathy index and have \(12\!-\!14\%\) charged and polar amino acids with respect to the artificial polypeptides (Table 1).

Given the difference in their primary structure, the physicochemical behavior of HELP and HELP1 is expected to be indicative of the role played by cross-linking domains in the observed phenomena.

The recombinant biopolymers were expressed and extracted as described24 and the purity was assessed by SDS-PAGE analysis that showed that no protein contaminants were present in the purified samples (Figure 2). HPLC profiles confirmed that samples did not contain contaminants, notably, no excluded compounds were detected.

Preliminary gravimetric evaluation of the aqueous solubility of each of the HELPs was performed to assess the concentration range that was suitable for experimental work. The initial dispersion of \(10~\mathrm{mg}\) of each biopolymer per mL of water was maintained at \(5\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and stirred for \(24~\mathrm{h}\); the samples were then centrifuged at \(9000\times\mathrm{g}\), and the supernatants were lyophilized. The solubility was found to be 4.4 and \(4.5~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) for HELP and HELP1, respectively. Based on these results, all of the experiments were performed on polypeptide solutions at concentrations below \(4.5~\mathrm{mg/mL}\).

The transition temperature has been suggested as a convenient parameter to describe the peculiar and distinctive thermal behavior of elastin-like polypeptides.34 Turbidity measurements were carried out to define the propensity of our recombinant products to undergo an inverse temperature transition. Because coacervation properties of human elastin-derived polypeptides have been studied mainly at \(\mathrm{NaCl}\)-concentrations higher than those that occur physiologically,21,34,35 we focused our attention on the inverse phase transition behavior of our macromolecules in conditions of low and near-physiological salt concentrations.

Solutions with different polypeptide concentrations were prepared for both HELP and HELP1. Each sample was monitored for turbidity as a function of temperature. The inverse transition temperature \((T_{\mathrm{t}})\) was the point corresponding to half of the maximum observed value. These values were then plotted against the biopolymer concentration (Figure 3). The analyses were performed in conditions of varying ionic strength, and different behavior for the two polypeptides was observed. In
![]('img_url')

Figure 3. Concentration dependence of transition temperatures $(T_{\mathrm{t}})$ for recombinant polypeptides. Turbidimetric analysis was performed in Tris buffered solution in the presence (A) or in the absence (B) of $0.15\,\mathsf{M}$ NaCl. HELP, squared symbols; and HELP1, rounded symbols. The error bar represents the standard deviations on three replicates.

the presence of \(0.15\,\mathrm{~M~}\,\mathrm{NaCl}\), the \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) of both polypeptides decreased as a function of the concentration (Figure 3A). In these conditions, both biopolymers showed inverse transition properties that closely resembled those widely described for other elastin-like polypeptides.9,21,36-38 In addition, the \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) of HELP was found to be equal to that of HELP1 at low biopolymer concentrations \((C_{\mathrm{biop}})\), but the \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) of HELP decreased at a faster rate as \(C_{\mathrm{biop}}\) increased \((\Delta T_{\mathrm{t}}=5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at the highest concentration). This indicates that the presence of cross-linking domains, such as those present in the HELP chain, favored the phase transition and likely improved the interactions among chains.

The observed behavior of the two biopolymers was completely different when salt-free solutions were assayed (Figure 3B). At the lowest \(C_{\mathrm{biop}}\), the HELP \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) \((25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) was remarkably lower than that of HELP1 \((44\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). Surprisingly, an opposite behavior of \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) of the two biopolymers was observed when the dependence on polypeptide concentration was evaluated. A monotonic increase in the \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) of the HELP polypeptide solutions was observed, with the highest rate mainly observed at low concentrations, whereas \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) of HELP1 exhibited the expected inverse relationship with concentration.

To investigate this unexpected observation for the HELP saltfree solutions, we performed further turbidimetric experiments at the lowest biopolymer concentration \((0.5\mathrm{~mg/mL}\) in Figure 3) at which the most striking difference was observed as a function of the NaCl concentration. The results reported in Figure 4A show that the salt concentration exerted a marked effect on HELP transition properties; the \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) increased as a function of the NaCl concentration. Moreover, at the physiological salt concentration, the turbidimetric profile became sharper and reached the highest turbidity value. In contrast, in the same conditions, HELP1 showed very sharp transition curves within a narrow temperature range, and a small decrease of \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) was observed with an increase in the salt concentration (Figure 4B).

