Figure 4. Effect of nearly physiological NaCl concentrations on diluted solutions $(0.5\;\mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{m L})$ of HELP (A) and HELP1 (B). Assay conditions were the absence of NaCl $(\boxed{\square},\bigcirc)$, 0.05 M NaCl (gray square, gray circle), and 0.15 M NaCl $(\blacksquare,\bullet)$.

These turbidimetric data clearly indicate that cross-linking domains affect the aggregation process because this phenomenon is observed for diluted solutions of HELP, but not for HELP1 in the same conditions. HELP aggregation in the absence of salt seemed to be somehow limited when compared to that occurring in conditions of physiological ionic strength. Furthermore, HELP aggregation particles formed in the absence and presence of physiological salt concentration were observed to have different features, as described previously.39 Although the presence of cross-linking domains favors chain interactions, it is likely that the hydrophobically driven phase separation may be hindered to some extent by electrostatic repulsion due to charged lysines. Thus, in these conditions, solvation of the chains and disassembly are favored. When this effect is dampened by salt addition, the full potential of the biopolymer for temperature-induced phase transition is restored.

The main difference between HELP and HELP1 is represented by the content in polar and charged amino acids, reflected also by the higher pI. In contrast, the two biopolymers show very close GRAVX values (Table 1). In this regard, it can be speculated that in the cross-linking domains the stretches of apolar alanine embed the lysines and this arrangement may somehow counteract the very polar character of lysines, maintaining the hydropathy profile of the whole macromolecule unchanged. This may be related to the different sensitivity to near-physiological salt concentration observed for the HELP biopolymers.

The dependence of the \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) on salt concentration over a wider range of NaCl concentrations \((0{-}1\,\mathrm{~M})\), was further explored for both biopolymers at two different concentrations (Table 2). At NaCl concentrations higher than that at physiological conditions, both HELP and HELP1 showed the expected regular decrease in the \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) as a function of salt concentration at the two biopolymer concentrations tested. Therefore, the physiological ionic strength was concluded to be a critical concentration that
