Calorimetric Analysis of HELP Biopolymers. Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) has been widely used to characterize the inverse phase transitions of elastin-like polypeptides.9,40,41 DSC thermograms of inverse transitions of HELP and HELP1 biopolymers in different NaCl concentrations are presented in Figure 5. The lowest salt concentrations at which the inverse transition could be detected were 0.15 and \(0.05\mathrm{~M~}\) for HELP and HELP1, respectively.

An endothermic peak with a marked asymmetry and a gradual tailing edge was recorded for both of the biopolymers. The peak area represented the enthalpy of transition \((\Delta H_{\mathrm{tr}})\) , and the inverse transition temperature was identified as the peak temperature \((T\mathrm{P^{k}})\) . As the salt concentration increased, the peaks for both of the polypeptides shifted gradually to lower temperatures, while the area significantly increased, as shown in Figure 5. As the salt concentration increased, both of the biopolymers showed a linear decrease of \(T\mathrm{P^{k}}\) , with a higher slope in the case of HELP as compared to HELP1 (Figure 6A).

