Secondary Structure Prediction and Circular Dichroism. On the basis of amino acid sequences, the distribution of secondary structures in the HELP and HELP1 chains were predicted using GOR IV and SOPMA programs. These two programs predicted an average value of \(26\%\) \(\upalpha\) -helix content for the HELP biopolymer due to the presence of the polyalanine segment that is localized in the cross-linking domains (Table 3, Figure 1). Conversely, using the GOR IV program, the hydrophobic domains for both of the polymers were predicted to assume a largely disordered structure (70 and \(93\%\) for HELP and HELP1, respectively) with small percentages of extended strands. A similar result was obtained using SOPMA, which provided an additional estimate of \(\beta\) -turn content. The map of the distribution of secondary structures obtained as output from the given amino acid sequences showed that \(\beta\) -turns were predicted to occur only in the hydrophobic regions for both of the polypeptides (data

not shown). As reported in Table 3, the sums of the percentages of disordered and \(\beta\) -turn structures (74 and \(99\%\) for HELP and HELP1, respectively), as well as the \(\upalpha\) -helix content, are very close to those obtained using GOR IV. For comparison, similar percentages were obtained for the synthetic polypeptides \((\mathrm{VPGVG})_{100}\) and \((\mathrm{VAPVG})_{90}\) and for bovine and human elastin. As shown in Table 3, the absence of stretches of alanine in the peptide sequence, such as in \((\mathrm{VPGVG})_{100}\) , \((\mathrm{VAPVG})_{90}\) , and HELP1 polypeptides, could determine the imperfect structuring \(\upalpha\) -helix domains that favor the \(\beta\) -turn sequences along the chain. Notably, the HELP sequence resembled the distribution of secondary structures predicted for human and bovine elastin, yielding \(20\!-\!23\%\) \(\upalpha\) -helix content and \(60\!-\!63\%\) unordered and \(\beta\) -turn structures.

CD spectra of \(0.1\ \mathrm{mg/mL}\) aqueous solutions of our recombinant biopolymers at three different temperatures below the \(T_{\mathrm{t}}\) (10, 23, and \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , from left to right) are presented in Figures 7 (HELP) and 8 (HELP1). The temperature values were chosen to detect any molecular rearrangement that precedes the inverse transition up to the physiological temperature. Measurements in different solvent conditions, specifically water and \(50\%\) TFE, were performed in the absence (panels \(\mathrm{A}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C})\) or in the presence of physiological salt concentration (panels \(\mathrm{D-F}\) ). Overall, the spectra of both biopolymers resembled those already described and reported for similar polypeptides49,35,45,46 and that of human tropoelastin.47 The spectra are characterized by a strong negative band centered near \(200\ \mathrm{nm}\) and a smaller negative shoulder at \(222{-}225\ \mathrm{nm}\) .

