reference databases of the deconvolution programs contain mainly spectra of globular proteins of known secondary structures and elastin peptides are not well represented.46 In general, in the CD spectra for elastin-like polypeptides, the negative band around \(200~\mathrm{nm}\) has been associated with coexistence of different conformer populations, which fluctuate among random coil, \(\beta\) -turn, and PPII secondary structures20,48 that have very similar conformational parameters in the Ramachandran map.49 The presence of additional negative bands at longer wavelengths has been extensively reported and is ascribed to \(\upalpha\) -helix domains \((222\ \mathrm{nm})^{35,50}\) as well as to type I/type II \(\beta\) -turn conformations \((225\ \mathrm{nm})\) .20,51

When the CD spectra of HELP in water (Figure 7) are compared with those of HELP1 (Figure 8), the main difference that is observed is the presence of a strong negative band at \(222\ \mathrm{nm}\) that occurs only in the HELP spectra. This feature is indicative of a marked propensity of HELP to adopt the \(\upalpha\) -helix conformation that is a consequence of the alanine-rich crosslinking domains. Thus, the CD profile of HELP resembles that of elastin,45,50 whereas the HELP1 profile seems more similar to those reported for exon 24 and for the permutations of the (VAPGVG) hexapeptidic motif.18,20 Both of the HELP and HELP1 polypeptides in aqueous solution exhibited an inner conformational stability up to the transition temperature. A slight reduction of the strong negative band around \(200\,\mathrm{\nm}\) was observed only in the presence of \(0.15\mathrm{~M~NaCl}\) at the higher temperature that was assayed (panels \(\mathrm{D-F}\) ).

The addition of TFE to aqueous elastin-like polypeptide solutions generally enhances \(\upalpha\) -helix domains in chain sequences with intrinsic helical propensity and favors \(\beta\) -type structures.52,53 As previously reported, TFE molecules are able to disrupt the structured water sphere surrounding the hydrophobic domain and form a solvent sphere around side chains, which favors local interactions and leads to the formation and enhancement of secondary structure.50,54 The \(50\%\) TFE concentration was chosen to generate the largest volume TFE solvation cluster around the amino acid side chains. Significant changes in the CD spectra were observed in the presence of TFE for both of the biopolymers in all of the conditions that were assayed (Figures 7 and 8). In the case of HELP, the drastic deepening of the 222 nm band after TFE addition suggested that alanine-rich se

quences existed partially in an \(\upalpha\) -helix conformation even in aqueous solution.32,55 Theoretical results presented in Table 3 partially support the more relevant \(\upalpha\) -helix structure that was observed by addition of TFE to HELP polypeptide solutions. In the case of HELP1, the primary result of TFE addition consisted of a marked reduction in the negative band around \(200\ \mathrm{\nm}\) and in the enhancement of the negative band at \(222{-}225\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 8), which suggests that an increase in folded conformations such as type I and type II \(\beta\) -turn with some \(\upalpha\) -helix contribution occurred. This behavior is reminiscent of that described for exon 24.20

Cell Culture on HELPs. HELPs, which are derived from human elastin, have the potential to be employed as cell culture substrates and the biopolymers were used to coat surfaces for cell culture. We used the well-studied human neuroblastoma SH-SY5Y cells as a cell model to test neuronal cell adhesion and response.

The well bottoms of a microtiter plate (96-well) were coated by evaporating an aqueous solution of each biopolymer to dryness (see Materials and Methods for details). We used both tissue culture treated and untreated polystyrene plates as positive and negative control surfaces for cell adhesion, respectively. The biopolymer material was found to be stable on the plate bottom after one week of incubation at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in PBS (see Supporting Information). The cells could not adhere on untreated polystyrene as expected (Figure 9 panel A). On the contrary, cells seeded on both HELP biopolymers (Figure 9, panels C and D) showed adhesion and proliferation features similar to those observed for cells grown on tissue culture treated polystyrene (Figure 9, panel B).

The cultures could be grown for up to two weeks, and changing the medium and the WST-1 assay confirmed cell viability (see Supporting Information). The SH-SY5Y cells are able to differentiate into a functional and morphological neuronal phenotype when treated with some compounds, such as retinoic acid and neurotrophic factors.56,57 We further evaluated if cells grown on HELP and HELP1 coatings maintained this feature. As is clearly shown in Figure 10, the SH-SY5Y cells cultured on both biopolymers, as well as cells grown on tissue culture
