These results are very encouraging for utilization of HELPs in neural tissue engineering, particularly in the context of surface modification and patterning that can control neural guidance.28,58,59

Data from our lab verify that, by controlling some conditions, such as salt concentration, well-ordered structures and regular patterns can be formed by our biopolymers, likely due to their self-assembling, temperature-dependent properties.60 Studies are currently underway to further investigate these aspects and explore the potential of HELPs as tools for engineering substrates to control cell behavior.

# Conclusions

In recent years, several studies on the propensity of elastinlike polypeptides for self-assembly through coacervation and on structure-property relationships have been carried out by several research groups. From these studies emerged that the tendency for self-assembly is strongly affected by the number of hydrophobic and cross-linking domains and by the peptide sequences.21,32,35,48

The two biopolymers produced in our lab have been shown to possess thermoresponsive properties derived from hydrophobic domains that are based on the VAPGVG motif that characterizes the human elastin; however, marked differences in the observed temperature-dependent behaviors emerged due to the presence of the alanine-lysine-rich cross-linking domains in only one of the recombinant products. HELP1, which is comprised of only the hydrophobic domains, showed a behavior very similar to that of the other well-described elastin-like polypeptides. In contrast, HELP, which better mimics the structure of natural elastin, showed some differences that were particularly evident when experiments were conducted in the absence of ionic strength.

The results presented in this work on the different thermal behavior of the two biopolymers in the dilute salt concentration range \((0{-}0.15\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{NaCl})\) allowed us to emphasize the effect of the presence of charged and polar groups on the HELP polypeptide, which disappeared at higher ionic strength as a consequence of charge screening effect. On the contrary, HELP1 was insensitive at changes in the ionic strength in the same range. Given the differences observed between the two biopolymers, it is likely that each one has inherent characteristics that make it more suitable for a specific application. For example, one process that is highly influenced by these features is the interaction among the chains when phase transition is triggered. Moreover, it has been shown that the self-assembly properties of this kind of polypeptides is dependent not only by the presence of the hydrophobic but also by the cross-linking domains.32 In particular, molecular assembly is often an important requirement in the context of surface engineering. In this work, water solutions of HELP and HELP1 at low ionic strength solution have been used in the coating procedure, resulting in a homogeneous distribution of the deposited material over the surface. Intriguingly, we have find that the self-assembling properties of the macromolecules can be exploited to achieve regular and ordered structures that enable spatial control, which is required in some specific approaches (e.g., micropatterning, controlled deposition). Under defined conditions, patterns on the mesoscale could be obtained and it has been observed that the behavior of the two macromolecules was rather different.61 The kinetic of the deposition mechanism underlying the process and the complexity of the biopolymer-surface interaction remain still unknown and will be the object of future investigations.

This specificity may be of significant interest if biopolymers are used as supports for cell culture. From this point of view, our data showed that both recombinant products support cell
