attachment and growth without detectable cytotoxicity. Moreover, cells maintained their phenotypic features and retained the potential to differentiate in response to proper stimuli. This preliminary characterization indicates that HELPs possess suitable features for use in many biomedical applications as scaffolds, vehicles for drug delivery and controlled release, and coatings.

Preparation of biomaterials based on HELPs by the use of stabilizing agents represents indeed an attractive option. Given the differences between the thermal behavior of HELP and HELP1, it can be expected that derived products with different features and properties could be obtained. Both polypeptides can be also easily modified by adding a specific domain inframe. In this way, in principle, their functionality can be further expanded, as well as that of any derived product.

There are several other advantages to using our biomacromolecules as biomaterials. These include the following: there

is no need to use organic solvents, as a nondenaturing aqueous environment is preferred. 3D scaffolds can be obtained by crosslinking to reproduce biomimetic environments; self-assembling under appropriate conditions could control the resulting structure at the micro- and nanoscale level.

The thermal responsiveness and cytocompatibility of the HELPs represent prototypes with highly desirable attributes. We envision further expansion of this family of tailored polypeptides that are showing promise, particularly as biomaterials able to mediate biological response at an interface.

